// Deterministic-encryption public-key encryption primitive: the
// randomness of Encrypt is an explicit argument, and KeyGen derives the
// key pair deterministically from a seed.
//
// This is the K-PKE layer of FIPS 203 (ML-KEM), Algorithms 13 to 15,
// abstracted away from any lattice structure:
//
//   K-PKE.KeyGen(d)          -> (ek_PKE, dk_PKE)   deterministic in d
//   K-PKE.Encrypt(ek, m, r)  -> c                  deterministic in (ek, m, r)
//   K-PKE.Decrypt(dk, c)     -> m                  deterministic
//
// The Fujisaki-Okamoto transform of FIPS 203 Algorithms 16 to 18 uses
// exactly this interface and nothing else about K-PKE: it re-encrypts
// with derandomized coins and compares ciphertexts. Modelling K-PKE as an
// abstract deterministic PKE therefore loses nothing for binding
// properties, which is why MLKEM.scheme is generic over this primitive.
//
// Ciphertexts, encapsulation keys, messages and randomness are byte
// strings of fixed length (BitString<N>, following this directory's
// convention that a BitString<N> models an N-byte value); only the
// decryption key is an opaque set, since nothing in the FO transform
// inspects it. Keeping ciphertexts and encapsulation keys as byte
// strings is what lets MLKEM implement the KEM primitive's injective
// Encode* methods as identities.
//
// The method signatures spell out BitString<...> rather than going
// through Set aliases so that every value's length is statically
// derivable; the engine's concatenation-equality decomposition needs
// that (see Hash.primitive's header).
//
// No correctness property is declared: the binding proof in
// proofs/MLKEM/ never needs Decrypt(dk, Encrypt(ek, m, r)) == m.
Primitive DetPKE(Int Nkseed, Int Nmsg, Int Nrand, Int Nct, Int Nek, Set DecKeySpace) {
    Int Nkseed = Nkseed;
    Int Nmsg = Nmsg;
    Int Nrand = Nrand;
    Int Nct = Nct;
    Int Nek = Nek;
    Set DecKey = DecKeySpace;

    deterministic [BitString<Nek>, DecKey] KeyGen(BitString<Nkseed> d);
    deterministic BitString<Nct> Encrypt(BitString<Nek> ek, BitString<Nmsg> m, BitString<Nrand> r);
    deterministic BitString<Nmsg> Decrypt(DecKey dk, BitString<Nct> ct);
}
